{"kind":"report","text":"Decision on 85f523f2 (planner, 2026-10-21): v2 directory fan-out.\n\n1. The finding stands, and it reaches past the leaves. By the fees in 85f523f2, a rewritten tree of n names costs 2,304n + 1,152 metadata. Two 1,000-name leaves cost 4,605,696, more than the 4 MiB budget. The ancestors have the same fault. In each namespace, `\u003ca\u003e/\u003cb\u003e/` lists 1,000 names once positions reach 1,000,000. Room A reaches that position in about seven months. So 100-file leaves under 1,000-name ancestors fail again at that point. Every numeric level needs a small fan-out. The recommended 100-file shard is not adopted.\n\n2. Adopted grammar. `\u003cfirst\u003e` stays 12 decimal digits, and the highest position stays 999,999,999,999. Each of its first eleven digits is one directory level:\n   - `journal/v2/passes/\u003cd1\u003e/.../\u003cd11\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e.json`\n   - `journal/v2/segments/\u003cd1\u003e/.../\u003cd11\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e.jsonl`\n   - `journal/v2/retained/\u003cd1\u003e/.../\u003cd11\u003e/\u003cfirst\u003e/\u003ckind\u003e/\u003cdigest\u003e`, with the `\u003cdomain\u003e` directory before the digest for a value, as now.\n   Each digit directory has a one-digit name, 0 to 9. So each tree on a numeric path lists at most 10 names, and so does each namespace directory.\n\n3. Retained paths. Only the digit path above `\u003cfirst\u003e` changes. A retained file stays in the directory of the pass that wrote it, and no later pass moves it. The `pass` column, rule 6 and rule 9 of 2.3 do not change. Witness 2's key from the pass at 3,700 keeps `pass` 3,700. Its path is now `retained/0/0/0/0/0/0/0/0/3/7/0/000000003700/...`.\n\n4. Cost bound. One rewritten tree of at most 10 names costs at most 24,192. One numeric path has 12 trees: the namespace directory and eleven digit directories. So one path costs at most 290,304, and all three namespaces cost at most 870,912. That is about a fifth of the metadata budget, at any position. This bound is arithmetic from the fees in 85f523f2. It is not a measurement. Rule 7 of 2.3 now says 10 names where it says 1,000. The caps, the debits, the append validation at send and the one ledger do not change. The same-pass validation credit is not adopted.\n\n5. Compatibility. No v2 archive exists, so no earlier v2 path needs a reader. v1 and its reader do not change. The conversion in 2.5 writes the v1 manifest, segments and retained files at the new digit paths for their positions. The retained files go to the directory of the pass from 0, as now.\n\n6. Reader. Rule 7 of 2.4 also refuses a directory under `passes/`, `segments/` or `retained/` whose name is not one digit. It refuses a file whose eleven directory digits differ from the first eleven digits of its `\u003cfirst\u003e`. The reader charges each tree that it reads to its work bound (rule 8). Each file adds at most 14 trees, so a namespace with N files has at most 14N + 1 trees. The other reader rules do not change.\n\n7. Witnesses. No native witness is needed before this decision. F1 adds two checks:\n   a. In witness 2, the tree probe asserts that each tree that a pass rewrites lists at most 10 names. The pass from 10,000 crosses a digit boundary at `\u003cd8\u003e`, and the probe also covers it.\n   b. A Git-layer test builds a parent tree in which each directory on the three paths for position 999,999,999,999 is full, with the files of positions 999,999,999,990 to 999,999,999,998 in place. A pass of one entry with one new retained input at that position fits the original budget. The test reports its tree work and metadata. It is Git-layer evidence only, with test-authored trees, and the F1 report labels it so.\n   Witness 3 also refuses a directory whose name is not one digit.\n\n8. The note. This decision amends sections 2.2, 2.3 rule 7, 2.4 rule 7, 2.5 item 4 and witnesses 2 and 3. A later planner head corrects the note's text, together with the witness 2 change in 18ef695b.\n\nNo deployment.","body":{"to":"builder"}}